Hello, and thanks for the report! Currently the design of this library is to avoid extra traits like `Default`, `PartialEq`, `Ord`, etc, for the types defined here. One reason is for codegen times (e.g. you probably don't need `Default` on _all_ the types) and another is that this is otherwise just intended to be a "zero cost abstraction" library over libc in terms of runtime and compile-time (e.g. a giant header file).

The good news, however, is that you can do initialization as you would in C most of the time. For example:

``` rust
let mut hints: libc::addrinfo = mem::zeroed();
hints.ai_family = af;
// ...

// or, "more unsafely"
let mut hints: libc::addrinfo = mem::uninitialized();
hints.ai_family = af;
// ...
```

Basically the idea is that you can create a generically zeroed version of the struct via `mem::zeroed` (which is similar to declaration + memset in C) or use `mem::uninitialized` (declaration with no memset). It's still a little unsafe, but you're guaranteed that there's no owned types in libc as all structs are `Copy` so in practice it should normally end up being "no more unsafe" than the FFI that's about to happen.

Hope that helps!

